dns-spoof

By: Steve deRosier
derosier@gmail.com

Copyright (C) 2010 Steve deRosier.
All rights reserved.

dns-spoof is a DNS server that only returns a hard-coded IP address for any A record queries.  At 
the moment it will return an IP of 6.6.6.6 for any lookups.

==
Building:

dns-spoof may be built by issuing the command `make`.  It should build on any basic Linux or MacOSX 
system.  

By default the makefile builds in debug mode.  To build in release, run `make RELEASE=1`.

A standard `make clean` will clean up the directory.

No install target was included as this program is not intended for distubution and use.

A test of the build can be run after building by using the command `make check`.

Typical building and use would be:
   make
   make check
   dns-spoof

Unit tests may be built by running `make` in the sub-directory test.  

==
dns-spoof usage

dns-spoof includes a number of command-line switches for ease of use or information printing:

   dns-spoof [options]
   
   Options:
    -c, --console   
       Don't daemonize, run in console mode.
    
    -h, --help      
       Display usage information.
    
    -p port, --port=port      
       Define the port to bind to.  By default, dns-spoof binds to port 53 for DNS.
    
    -v, --version   
       Display the version information.

Basic use can be as simple as the following:
./dns-spoof -p 9797
dig @localhost -p 9797 textureandlight.com


==
Notes and limitations:

dns-spoof is not intended to be used as a real DNS server.  It follows the RFC enough to work with 
dig for its limited purpose, but is not fully implmented.  Unusal datagrams, different types of
lookups and so on won't work.

It doesn't implment message compression.  It wasn't needed in the limited scope of the assignment.

Only UDP sockets are implmented.  There was no need to implment other socket types.

Only enough of DNS messages are implmented to handle the limited assignment.
